WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 108: King Kamehameha I: The Unification of the Hawaiian Islands

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.760
[Rebecca] Welcome back to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:06.960
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:22.640
[Rebecca] Today, we journey across the vast Pacific Ocean to the Paradise Islands of Hawaii during the late 18th century to witness the rise of one of the greatest warrior kings and nation builders in planetary history, King Kamehameha I, known to history as Kamehameha the Great.

4
00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:31.600
[Rebecca] Born on the Kohala coast under the blazing omen of Halle's comet, Kamehameha was prophesied to become the slayer of kings and unifier of the islands.

5
00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:45.040
[Rebecca] Standing six foot six with legendary physical strength, he overturned the immovable Fortun Naha Stone and waged a brilliant, 20-year military campaign, combining traditional Polynesian war canoes with Western muskets and cannons.

6
00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:56.640
[Rebecca] By 1810, he had forged the independent kingdom of Hawaii and established the Kanawai Mamalaho, the law of the splintered paddle, protecting the human rights of ordinary travelers forever.

7
00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:02.640
[Rebecca] Here is George with the full magnificent story.

8
00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:09.280
[George] In the Hawaiian language, the name Kamehaimha translates to the Lonely One, or the one set apart.

9
00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:18.400
[George] According to ancient Hawaiian oral chants, he was born around 1758 on the rugged northern Kohala coast of Hawaii Island, the Big Island.

10
00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:25.920
[George] His birth coincided with the dazzling, terrifying appearance of a brilliant celestial traveler streaking across the night sky.

11
00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:41.920
[George] Hala's comet, recognized by Hawaiian Kahuna priests as a cosmic omen announcing the birth of an extraordinary high chief who would conquer all rivals, overthrow the old dynasties, and unite the Hawaiian archipelago under a single sovereign rule.

12
00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:50.000
[George] Terrified by the prophecy of a newborn who would slay existing monarchs, reigning High Chief Alape ordered the newborn infant killed.

13
00:01:50.000 --> 00:02:04.600
[George] To save the child's life, his mother, Kekuyapoa, smuggled the baby into the remote mountain forests of Awini, where he was hidden in caves and raised in secret by trusted foster parents for five years until the political danger subsided.

14
00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:11.560
[George] Returning to court as a young man, Kamehameha grew into an imposing, intimidating physical giant.

15
00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:26.280
[George] Standing six feet six inches tall with broad, muscular shoulders and an intense, piercing gaze, he was trained in the elite martial art of Lua, the ancient Hawaiian combat discipline of bone breaking, spear dodging, and wrestling.

16
00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:37.800
[George] He served as the trusted lieutenant and master warrior to his uncle, King Kalaneapo, displaying extraordinary courage and battlefield composure in clashes against rival Maui chiefs.

17
00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:49.080
[George] Ancient Hawaiian oral traditions preserved the genealogical chants of the great Ali, celebrating ancestral warriors whose mana was woven into the sacred volcanic soil of the islands.

18
00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:58.360
[George] From boyhood, he learned the sacred chants of navigation, reading the ocean swells and flight patterns of seabirds to journey between island kingdoms.

19
00:02:58.360 --> 00:03:08.680
[George] In the sacred precinct of Hilo stood the legendary Naha Stone, a colossal, semi-sacred basalt boulder weighing an estimated 4,500 pounds.

20
00:03:08.680 --> 00:03:15.000
[George] Ancient prophecy held that only chiefs of the exalted Naha royal lineage were permitted to sit upon the stone.

21
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:25.560
[George] And an ancient Kapo proclaimed that any warrior who possessed the superhuman strength to overturn the Naha stone would become the conqueror and sovereign ruler of all the Hawaiian islands.

22
00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:35.080
[George] Around 1780, while visiting Hilo, the young Kamehameha approached the sacred stone before an assembly of skeptical priests and rival chiefs.

23
00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:44.600
[George] Kamehaimha laid his hands upon the colossal volcanic boulder, braced his massive legs against the earth, and channeled his mana, spiritual energy.

24
00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:57.600
[George] Straining with every sinew of his immense frame until blood vessels bulged across his temples, Camehamha lifted the immovable stone from its volcanic cradle and flipped it completely over onto its opposite face.

25
00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:02.320
[George] The priests fell to their knees in awe, and shock rippled across the islands.

26
00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:04.400
[George] The prophecy had been fulfilled.

27
00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:11.280
[George] Shortly thereafter, upon King Kalaneapo's death in 1782, the kingdom of the Big Island was divided.

28
00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:26.560
[George] The king's son, Kiwilao, inherited the royal throne and civil lands, but in a profound spiritual act, the king entrusted young Kamehameha with the guardianship of Kykuilamoku, the feathered, snarling war god of the Hawaiian kings.

29
00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:28.640
[George] Civil war was inevitable.

30
00:04:28.640 --> 00:04:39.440
[George] Witnesses recounted that Camehaimha's veins stood out like corded rope as he hoisted the 4,000-pound basalt monolith, forever changing the political destiny of Hawaii.

31
00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:47.360
[George] The royal priests recognized that overturning the volcanic boulder fulfilled the ancient prophecies recorded in sacred temple chants.

32
00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:53.200
[George] Civil war erupted in July 1782 at the Battle of Mokohai on the Kona coast.

33
00:04:53.200 --> 00:05:05.680
[George] In a day of fierce hand-to-hand combat fought with Koawood daggers, shark tooth clubs and heavy throwing spears, Cameha's warriors emerged victorious, and King Kilau was slain on the field.

34
00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:15.040
[George] Camehaimha established firm control over the western districts of Kohala, Kona, and Hamakua, while rival chiefs held Hilo and Cau.

35
00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:24.560
[George] Recognizing that conventional Polynesian weaponry alone could not conquer the outer islands, Camehamha displayed extraordinary strategic flexibility.

36
00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:40.840
[George] In 1790, following a violent maritime incident with American merchant ships, Kamehameha captured the small American schooner Fair American and spared the lives of two stranded British sailors, Master Gunner John Young and boatswain Isaac Davis.

37
00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:53.080
[George] Rather than treating Young and Davis as captives, Kamehameha elevated them to honored royal advisors, granting them high chief status, vast estates, and Hawaiian wives of noble birth.

38
00:05:53.080 --> 00:05:57.960
[George] Young and Davis proved fiercely loyal to Kamehameha for the rest of their lives.

39
00:05:57.960 --> 00:06:06.920
[George] They trained Kamehameha's warriors in the maintenance and tactical deployment of flintlock muskets, iron swivel guns, and brass field cannons.

40
00:06:06.920 --> 00:06:16.120
[George] Kamehameha assembled an elite arsenal of European weaponry that gave his military an overwhelming technological advantage over rival chiefs.

41
00:06:16.120 --> 00:06:27.880
[George] John Young and Isaac Davis became trusted royal advisers, establishing artillery battery placements and advising Camehama on European diplomatic customs and maritime navigation.

42
00:06:27.880 --> 00:06:36.600
[George] European brass cannons mounted aboard Hawaiian war canoes created an invincible naval battery that swept across the Choppy Island channels.

43
00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:46.760
[George] By late 1790, Camehama's primary rival for control of the big island was his cousin, Kiwa Ka Hayula, ruler of the southern Cao District.

44
00:06:46.760 --> 00:06:58.520
[George] The two armies clashed indecisively in the Wapio Valley, and Kiwa marched his warriors southward back toward Cao, following a trail that skirted the summit caldera of Kiluya volcano.

45
00:06:58.520 --> 00:07:08.840
[George] As Kiwa's army marched past the volcano in three divisions, Peel, the Hawaiian goddess of fire and volcanoes, intervened with apocalyptic fury.

46
00:07:08.840 --> 00:07:20.400
[George] Kiluya erupted in a violent freatomagmatic explosion, blasting a colossal column of superheated steam, pulverized rock, and suffocating sulfur gas miles into the sky.

47
00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:27.360
[George] A dense, incandescent pyroclastic cloud swept across the desert, engulfing Kiwa's middle division.

48
00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:40.160
[George] Over 400 warriors, women, and children were asphyxiated instantly in the volcanic blast, their footprints preserved in hardened ash layers that remain visible today in Hawaii Volcano's National Park.

49
00:07:40.160 --> 00:07:44.720
[George] Across the islands, the disaster was hailed as a clear sign from heaven.

50
00:07:44.720 --> 00:07:55.280
[George] Peel herself had endorsed Kamehameha as the chosen unifier, breaking Kiwa's spiritual morale and paving the way for Kamehameha's total consolidation of Hawaii Island.

51
00:07:55.280 --> 00:08:05.440
[George] The catastrophic pyroclastic surge from Kiluya convinced even the most hardened warriors that the elemental gods of the earth had chosen Kamehameha to rule the archipelago.

52
00:08:05.440 --> 00:08:14.400
[George] The footprints of Kiwa's doomed warriors remain frozen in volcanic ash, preserved for centuries as a sombre monument to divine intervention.

53
00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:21.920
[George] With the big island united, Camehama turned his eyes across the ocean toward Maui, Malokai, and Oahu.

54
00:08:21.920 --> 00:08:36.320
[George] He constructed an immense naval armada of over 1200 war canoes, including massive double hulled catamarans mounting brass cannon in their prows, supplemented by European style schooners built by foreign shipwrights under his command.

55
00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:44.240
[George] In early 1795, Kamehameha's armada swept across the islands, easily overrunning Maui and Malokai.

56
00:08:44.240 --> 00:08:52.240
[George] In April 1795, an army of over 10,000 elite warriors landed on the beaches of Waikiki and Kahala on Oahu.

57
00:08:52.240 --> 00:09:02.360
[George] Facing him was the Oahu chief Kalinikupul, who had fortified positions in Nuyuanu Valley behind stonewall redoubts and cannons operated by foreign deserters.

58
00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:06.200
[George] The decisive battle of Nuanuanu was fought with savage intensity.

59
00:09:06.440 --> 00:09:16.920
[George] Camehaimha's warriors, supported by artillery fire directed by John Young, drove the Oahu forces relentlessly up the steep, narrowing volcanic valley.

60
00:09:16.920 --> 00:09:28.920
[George] The retreat became a desperate panic as Kalinikupule's soldiers were trapped against the sheer, vertical precipice of the Nuyuanu Pali, a thousand-foot sheer cliff dropping into the windward plains below.

61
00:09:28.920 --> 00:09:36.600
[George] Rather than surrender, hundreds of Oahu warriors were driven over the edge, plunging to their deaths on the jagged rocks below.

62
00:09:36.600 --> 00:09:41.000
[George] The conquest of Oahu made Camhamha master of the Central Islands.

63
00:09:41.000 --> 00:09:52.120
[George] The sheer precipice of the Nuanuanu Pali became the final, tragic battleground of the conquest of Oahu, sealing Kamehameha's absolute military dominion over the Central Islands.

64
00:09:52.120 --> 00:10:00.760
[George] The wind howling through Nuanuanu Pass carried the final war cries of Oahu defenders as Kamehameha stood victorious on the windswept ridge.

65
00:10:00.760 --> 00:10:05.000
[George] Yet Kamehameha was far more than a ruthless military conqueror.

66
00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:10.040
[George] He was an enlightened, compassionate lawgiver who sought to heal the wounds of civil war.

67
00:10:10.040 --> 00:10:18.760
[George] The foundational moral law of his kingdom was born from a dramatic personal incident that occurred in 1783, long before his conquest.

68
00:10:18.760 --> 00:10:24.600
[George] During a coastal raid in Pune, Camehamha pursued two poor fishermen down a rocky beach.

69
00:10:24.600 --> 00:10:29.800
[George] In the chase, Camehameha's foot became wedged in a crevice of the jagged lava reef.

70
00:10:29.800 --> 00:10:33.160
[George] Trapped and unable to move, he was vulnerable.

71
00:10:33.160 --> 00:10:45.000
[George] One of the fishermen, fearing for his family's life, picked up a heavy wooden canoe paddle and struck Kamehameha forcefully across the head, breaking the paddle into splinters, before escaping with his companion.

72
00:10:45.600 --> 00:10:54.720
[George] Years later, when Camehamha had become king, the two fishermen were captured and brought before him in chains, expecting immediate execution.

73
00:10:54.720 --> 00:11:04.560
[George] Instead, King Kamehameha publicly admitted his own wrongdoing, declaring that he had used unlawful force against peaceful civilians engaged in lawful labor.

74
00:11:04.560 --> 00:11:11.760
[George] He pardoned the men, granted them land, and promulgated the Kanawai Mamalaho, the law of the splintered paddle.

75
00:11:11.760 --> 00:11:16.640
[George] O my people, honor your God, respect alike the great and the humble.

76
00:11:16.640 --> 00:11:23.120
[George] See to it that our aged are women and our children may walk by the roadside and lie down to sleep in peace.

77
00:11:23.120 --> 00:11:25.120
[George] And let none molest them.

78
00:11:25.120 --> 00:11:35.520
[George] It was the world's first formal human rights declaration protecting noncombatants during war, and remains enshrined today in Article IX of the Hawaii state constitution.

79
00:11:35.520 --> 00:11:45.680
[George] The law of the splintered paddle established that true leadership is rooted in humility, justice, and the sacred obligation of the ruler to protect the humble and defenseless.

80
00:11:45.680 --> 00:11:55.040
[George] The immortal words of the Kanawai Mamalaho established universal human rights in Hawaii nearly two centuries before the United Nations Declaration.

81
00:11:55.040 --> 00:12:01.200
[George] By 1810, only the northwestern island of Kawei remained outside Kamehameha's grasp.

82
00:12:01.200 --> 00:12:12.400
[George] Twice Kamehameha prepared colossal invasion fleets to conquer Kahoe, but the first was turned back by a howling ocean tempest, and the second was halted by a deadly foreign epidemic.

83
00:12:12.400 --> 00:12:20.880
[George] Recognizing that further war would merely devastate his people, King Kamuali of Kawe wisely negotiated a diplomatic compromise.

84
00:12:20.880 --> 00:12:26.000
[George] He voluntarily placed Kawe under Kamehameha's sovereignty as a tributary vassal.

85
00:12:26.000 --> 00:12:30.000
[George] Completing the peaceful unification of all eight Hawaiian islands.

86
00:12:30.200 --> 00:12:42.200
[George] For the remainder of his reign, King Kamehameha governed the United Kingdom of Hawaii with profound wisdom, maintaining national sovereignty against British, American, and Russian imperial ambitions.

87
00:12:42.200 --> 00:12:54.840
[George] He established a thriving international trade in sandalwood, encouraged agriculture, preserved traditional cultural kapo religious systems, and ensured that foreign visitors respected Hawaiian laws.

88
00:12:54.840 --> 00:13:02.440
[George] King Kamehameha passed away on May 8, 1819, at Kailuakona at the age of approximately 61.

89
00:13:02.440 --> 00:13:14.200
[George] In accordance with sacred ancient custom, his trusted high advisor hopily concealed the king's bones in a secret mountain cave, ensuring that no enemy could ever desecrate his sacred mana.

90
00:13:14.200 --> 00:13:17.080
[George] A traditional Hawaiian chant proclaims.

91
00:13:17.080 --> 00:13:21.080
[George] Only the stars of the heavens know the resting place of Kamehameha.

92
00:13:21.080 --> 00:13:34.440
[George] Today, monumental gilded bronze statues of Kamehameha the Great stand proudly in Honolulu and in the United States capital, an eternal symbol of indigenous leadership, nationhood, and majestic Hawaiian pride.

93
00:13:34.440 --> 00:13:45.320
[George] Kamehameha's unyielding diplomatic resolve protected the nascent Hawaiian kingdom from European colonial subjugation, inaugurating a century of sovereign Pacific nationhood.

94
00:13:45.320 --> 00:13:55.960
[George] Today, Hawaiian citizens across the globe honor Kamehameha Day with grand parades and floral lay ceremonies, celebrating the founder of the Hawaiian nation.

95
00:14:07.400 --> 00:14:08.760
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

96
00:14:08.760 --> 00:14:17.040
[Rebecca] Join us next week for our series, Finale in the Fifty States, as we enter Ayolani Palace for the heartbreaking story of Queen Lady Oakalani.

